## Background and Motivation

The property `PublicKey.Key` is not recommended for use since it does not handle EC key types (and it cannot since the algorithm is ambiguous between `ECDsa` and `ECDiffieHellman`). We should consider similar APIs that are on `X509Certificate2` to get the correct public key algorithm instance.

## Proposed API

```diff
namespace System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates
{
    public class PublicKey
    {
+       public RSA? GetRSAPublicKey();
+       public ECDsa? GetECDsaPublicKey();
+       public DSA? GetDSAPublicKey();
+       public ECDiffieHellman? GetECDiffieHellmanPublicKey();
    }
}
```

## Usage Examples

Similar to how `X509Certificate2` works (except there some of these are extension methods):

```csharp
if (publicKey.GetRSAPublicKey() is RSA rsa) {
    //..
}
else if (publicKey.GetECDsaPublicKey() is ECDsa ecdsa) {
    //..
}
```

## Alternative Designs

This can be achieved with some more verbose code (but not necessarily intuitive). Something along the lines of:

```csharp
byte[] spki = publicKey.ExportSubjectPublicKeyInfo();

if (iWantAnRsaKey) {
    Debug.Assert(publicKey.Oid.Value == "1.2.840.113549.1.1.1");
    using RSA rsa = RSA.Create();
    rsa.ImportSubjectPublicKeyInfo(spki, out _);
}
else if (iWantAnECDsaKey) {
    Debug.Assert(publicKey.Oid.Value == "1.2.840.10045.2.1");
    using ECDsa ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
    ecdsa.ImportSubjectPublicKeyInfo(spki, out _);
}
```

## Risks

No particular risks.

